articles.content_en:
The Standing Committee of the National People's Congress, having discussed the meaning of the issue concerning 'having the ability to execute but refusing to do so, and the circumstances are serious' as stipulated in Article 313 of the Criminal Law, hereby interprets as follows:
The 'judgments and rulings of a People's Court' as provided in Article 313 of the Criminal Law refer to judgments and rulings that are made by a People's Court according to law, have enforcement content, and have taken legal effect. Rulings made by a People's Court for the enforcement according to law of payment orders, effective mediation statements, arbitration awards, notarized instruments of debt, etc., shall fall within the scope of rulings provided in that Article.
The following circumstances shall constitute the circumstances of 'having the ability to execute but refusing to do so, and the circumstances are serious' as provided in Article 313 of the Criminal Law:
(1) The person subject to execution conceals, transfers, intentionally damages or destroys property, or transfers property without compensation, or transfers property at a manifestly unreasonably low price, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling impossible to enforce;
(2) The surety or the person subject to execution conceals, transfers, intentionally damages or destroys, or transfers property that has been provided as security to the People's Court, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling impossible to enforce;
(3) The person obliged to assist in execution, after receiving a notice of assistance in execution from the People's Court, refuses to assist in execution, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling impossible to enforce;
(4) The person subject to execution, the surety, or the person obliged to assist in execution colludes with a functionary of a State organ, exploiting the authority of the functionary of the State organ to obstruct enforcement, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling impossible to enforce;
(5) Other circumstances where the person has the ability to execute but refuses to do so, and the circumstances are serious.
Where a functionary of a State organ commits the act specified in item (4) above, he shall be investigated for criminal liability as an accomplice in the crime of refusing to execute a judgment or ruling. Where a functionary of a State organ accepts bribes or abuses his authority, and commits the act specified in item (4) above, which concurrently constitutes the crime provided in Article 385 (crime of accepting bribes) or Article 397 (crime of abuse of authority; crime of dereliction of duty) of the Criminal Law, he shall be convicted and punished in accordance with the provisions that impose the heavier punishment.
This is hereby announced.
